{
  "image_name": "09f131e15f1cac5b94a4038a07ad7b42.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how an Ag–C interlayer changes lithium deposition behavior in a solid-state battery. Across the top comparisons, a conventional stack with an NMC cathode, solid electrolyte, and stainless-steel current collector develops a thick, uneven plated lithium layer during Li plating, which appears to grow rougher and more irregular after cycling. In contrast, when a thin Ag–C anode layer is added at the interface, lithium plates beneath this layer in a much flatter, more confined way, and the interface remains comparatively stable after repeated cycling.\n\nThe lower schematic explains the proposed mechanism. During charging, lithium ions move through the solid electrolyte and react with dispersed Ag particles in the carbon network, forming Li–Ag alloy regions. These alloy sites guide lithium nucleation and deposition underneath the interlayer, producing a smoother Li–Ag plated region. During discharging, lithium is stripped back out, while the Ag particles remain distributed in the carbon framework and can continue to host reversible alloying. Overall, the image suggests that the Ag–C layer promotes uniform lithium plating/stripping, suppresses interfacial roughening, and supports thicker but controlled alloy deposits.",
